𝗜𝗻𝘁𝗲𝗿𝘃𝗶𝗲𝘄 𝗤𝘂𝗲𝘀𝘁𝗶𝗼𝗻:

👉 “Can you explain Kubernetes architecture and how its components work together?”

When I started learning Kubernetes, I realized that memorizing component names wasn't enough. Understanding how they communicate makes troubleshooting much easier.

A Kubernetes cluster has two major parts:

🔹 𝗖𝗼𝗻𝘁𝗿𝗼𝗹 𝗣𝗹𝗮𝗻𝗲 — 𝗧𝗵𝗲 𝗕𝗿𝗮𝗶𝗻

It manages the cluster and makes decisions.

• API Server – Entry point for Kubernetes API requests.
• etcd – Stores cluster state and configuration.
• Scheduler – Selects a suitable node for unscheduled Pods.
• Controller Manager – Runs controllers that work to maintain the desired state.
• Cloud Controller Manager – Integrates with supported cloud infrastructure.

🔹 𝗪𝗼𝗿𝗸𝗲𝗿 𝗡𝗼𝗱𝗲𝘀 — 𝗪𝗵𝗲𝗿𝗲 𝗔𝗽𝗽𝗹𝗶𝗰𝗮𝘁𝗶𝗼𝗻𝘀 𝗥𝘂𝗻

• Kubelet – Ensures Pods are running as specified.
• Container Runtime – Runs containers.
• Kube-proxy – Implements Service networking rules on nodes in clusters that use it.

🎯 𝗛𝗼𝘄 𝗗𝗼 𝗧𝗵𝗲𝘆 𝗪𝗼𝗿𝗸 𝗧𝗼𝗴𝗲𝘁𝗵𝗲𝗿?

Imagine deploying an application to Kubernetes:

1️⃣ You submit a Deployment manifest through the API Server.

2️⃣ The desired state is stored in etcd.

3️⃣ Controllers work to create the required resources.

4️⃣ The Scheduler assigns Pods to suitable nodes.

5️⃣ The Kubelet on each node works with the container runtime to start the containers.

6️⃣ Kubernetes continuously works to maintain the desired state.

💡 𝗧𝗵𝗲 𝗦𝗶𝗺𝗽𝗹𝗲 𝗪𝗮𝘆 𝗜 𝗥𝗲𝗺𝗲𝗺𝗯𝗲𝗿 𝗜𝘁:

👉 Control Plane = Makes decisions and manages the cluster.

👉 Worker Nodes = Run the application workloads.

Understanding this flow helps connect Pods, Nodes, Scheduling, Networking, and Container Runtime into one picture.

𝗞𝗲𝘆 𝗟𝗲𝗮𝗿𝗻𝗶𝗻𝗴:

Don't just memorize Kubernetes components. Understand what each component does and what happens when it fails.
